---
import ProjectLayout from "../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro";

const workflow = [
  ["01", "Validate the inputs", "Check keys, missing values, token counts, rates, and relationships before calculating results."],
  ["02", "Respect the grain", "Aggregate prompt costs to each campaign and task before joining task hours. Roll up to the campaign before joining revenue and other spending, so repeated prompts cannot multiply time or revenue."],
  ["03", "Separate the measures", "Keep token spend, Net AI value, ROI, and campaign result distinct so each answers a different question."],
  ["04", "Challenge the baseline", "Move estimated no-AI hours by ±20%, hold observed hours and AI costs fixed, and check whether the decision changes."],
];
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="AI Cost Efficiency & Workflow Value"
  description="A portfolio project I created for fellow learners to download and follow along with. Explore a fictional agency's AI costs, build your own analysis, and turn your findings into a piece for your own portfolio. My completed dashboard and solutions are available when you're ready to compare."
  eyebrow="Downloadable portfolio project · Two learner tracks"
  emoji="↗"
  repo="https://github.com/GlassHouse97/AI-Cost-Efficiency-Portfolio-Project"
  accent="#79e0ba"
  primaryHref="#learn"
  primaryLabel="Download & follow along"
>
  <a slot="hero-action" class="button" href="https://ai-cost-efficiency-860922042669.us-east1.run.app" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">View my completed dashboard <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>

  <div slot="hero-visual" class="outcome-board">
    <div class="board-heading"><span>Two campaigns in focus.</span><small>Synthetic data</small></div>
    <p class="board-question">Is the time worth<br /><em>the token spend?</em></p>
    <div class="outcome-row positive">
      <span><b>Campaign 1</b><small>100 hours saved</small></span>
      <strong>+$4,500<small>Net AI value</small></strong>
    </div>
    <div class="outcome-row negative">
      <span><b>Campaign 2</b><small>70 extra hours</small></span>
      <strong>−$4,900<small>Net AI value</small></strong>
    </div>
    <p class="board-note">At $50/hour, after AI cost. Time value is not cash savings.</p>
  </div>

  <div slot="stats" class="stat"><strong>4 campaigns</strong><span>Fictional source dataset</span></div>
  <div slot="stats" class="stat"><strong>$50/hour</strong><span>Assumed time value</span></div>
  <div slot="stats" class="stat"><strong>±20%</strong><span>No-AI baseline test</span></div>
  <div slot="stats" class="stat"><strong>2 tracks</strong><span>Learn the analysis</span></div>

  <section class="project-section">
    <div><span class="section-number">01</span><h2>The business question</h2></div>
    <div class="body-copy">
      <p>A business question you can turn into your own portfolio project.</p>
      <p>I built this as both a demonstration of my work and a project other learners could take on. You get the source data, business questions, and instructions. Choose your tools, work through the analysis, and publish your own findings and recommendations in your portfolio.</p>
      <p>I wanted to look past the AI bill and ask what happened to the work itself. This project counts preparation, prompting, review, and rework, then tests whether the estimated time value still covers the fees when the no-AI baseline changes.</p>
      <div class="callout"><strong>A fictional business case.</strong> Marketing R Us and ChatABC are fictional. All records and prices were created for this exercise. The live dashboard includes the completed analysis.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="project-section">
    <div><span class="section-number">02</span><h2>How the model works</h2></div>
    <div class="body-copy">
      <p>The calculation starts at the prompt and ends at a business decision.</p>
      <div class="workflow-grid">
        {workflow.map(([number, title, description]) => (
          <article><span>{number}</span><h3>{title}</h3><p>{description}</p></article>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p>A small AI bill can look like a win until you count the time spent checking and fixing the output. I wanted to see whether each campaign got enough useful work back to justify the cost, and how the campaign performed overall. These five measures help answer those questions.</p>
      <ul class="kpi-list">
        <li><strong>AI cost.</strong> What the prompts cost, based on the tokens sent and received and the fictional model prices in the dataset.</li>
        <li><strong>Hours saved.</strong> How actual working time compares with the estimate for doing the same work without AI. Actual time includes preparation, prompting, review, and rework. A negative result means the work took longer.</li>
        <li><strong>Net AI value.</strong> The estimated value of the time gained or lost, after accounting for AI fees.</li>
        <li><strong>AI ROI.</strong> The estimated net value relative to what was spent on AI, shown as a percentage. It helps compare returns on AI spending across campaigns and is separate from the campaign's profit margin.</li>
        <li><strong>Campaign result.</strong> How much campaign revenue remains after accounting for staff time, AI fees, and other campaign spending. This looks at the campaign as a whole.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>I value staff time at $50 an hour for this exercise. Time saved can free people up for other work, but it doesn't mean payroll costs fell. The no-AI hours are estimates, so these results help compare scenarios rather than prove AI caused the difference.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="project-section">
    <div><span class="section-number">03</span><h2>What the example shows</h2></div>
    <div class="body-copy">
      <p>Campaign 1 supports continued use. Campaign 2 needs a closer look at the workflow.</p>
      <p>The four campaigns produce $2,820 of estimated net AI value in total, but that average hides very different outcomes. Campaigns 1 and 2 provide the focal comparison. Staff time explains $8,500 of their $9,400 value gap, while AI fees explain the remaining $900.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Synthetic campaign results" tabindex="0">
        <table>
          <caption>Fixed fictional scenario · $50 per hour</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Measure</th><th scope="col">Campaign 1</th><th scope="col">Campaign 2</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Hours saved</th><td class="positive">100 h</td><td class="negative">−70 h</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">AI cost</th><td>$500</td><td>$1,400</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Net AI value</th><td class="positive">+$4,500</td><td class="negative">−$4,900</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">AI ROI</th><td class="positive">900%</td><td class="negative">−350%</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Campaign result</th><td class="positive">$7,500</td><td class="negative">−$2,400</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Campaign 1's 100 saved hours have an assumed value of $5,000; subtracting $500 of AI spend leaves $4,500. Campaign 2's 70 additional hours cost $3,500 in modeled time before its $1,400 AI spend is included.</p>
      <div class="sensitivity-panel">
        <span class="mini-label">Sensitivity · no-AI hours ±20%</span>
        <h3>The signs hold across the tested range.</h3>
        <div class="range-row"><b>Campaign 1</b><span class="positive">+$2,100 → +$6,900</span></div>
        <div class="range-row"><b>Campaign 2</b><span class="negative">−$6,900 → −$2,900</span></div>
        <p>Ranges show Net AI value while actual hours and AI costs stay fixed. This is a scenario test, not a confidence interval or proof of causality.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="project-section">
    <div><span class="section-number">04</span><h2>Three recommendations</h2></div>
    <div class="body-copy">
      <ol class="recommendations">
        <li><strong>Expand Campaign 1-style use selectively.</strong> Its modeled result stays positive in the sensitivity test. Pilot comparable work and check delivery quality before extending the conclusion.</li>
        <li><strong>Redesign the Campaign 2 workflow before scaling it.</strong> Inspect costly tasks, repeated prompts, and rework. Test changes against a recorded baseline instead of assuming a cheaper model alone fixes the problem.</li>
        <li><strong>Measure outcomes alongside spend.</strong> Track actual effort, review and correction time, output quality, and revenue. Revisit the no-AI baseline and keep time value separate from realized cash savings.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="button-row">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="https://ai-cost-efficiency-860922042669.us-east1.run.app" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Explore the dashboard <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        <a class="button" href="https://github.com/GlassHouse97/AI-Cost-Efficiency-Portfolio-Project" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Inspect the analysis <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="project-section" id="learn">
    <div><span class="section-number">05</span><h2>Try the analysis yourself</h2></div>
    <div class="body-copy">
      <p>Download the case. Work through it. Make it a portfolio piece of your own.</p>
      <p>Both tracks include an Excel workbook, CSV source files, and full instructions. Extract the ZIP and open <strong>START_HERE.html</strong>. The learner packages contain the questions and data so you can build your own answer before looking at mine.</p>
      <ol class="learner-steps">
        <li><strong>Choose a track.</strong> Pick the shorter Excel challenge or the full portfolio project.</li>
        <li><strong>Follow the assignment.</strong> Clean the data, calculate the costs, and test the assumptions with the tools you want to practice.</li>
        <li><strong>Build your own result.</strong> Create a workbook, dashboard, or written analysis that explains what you found.</li>
        <li><strong>Add it to your portfolio.</strong> Link the source exercise, explain your approach, and show your own conclusions.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="track-grid">
        <article class="track-card">
          <span class="mini-label">Beginner · About 4 hours</span>
          <h3>Quick Challenge</h3>
          <p>Start with 120 tasks from two campaigns. Use Excel or a BI tool to calculate AI costs, compare results, and build a concise recommendation you can share.</p>
          <span class="track-note">Direct download · No website account</span>
          <a class="button" href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GlassHouse97/AI-Cost-Efficiency-Portfolio-Project/main/DATA/TRACKS/quick-challenge/mru-quick-challenge.zip">Download quick challenge <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
        </article>
        <article class="track-card">
          <span class="mini-label">Deeper analysis · 12–16 hours</span>
          <h3>Portfolio Project</h3>
          <p>Explore 240 tasks and 3,360 prompts across four campaigns. Use Excel, SQL, Python, or BI to investigate workflow value and create your own dashboard or case study.</p>
          <span class="track-note">Direct download · No website account</span>
          <a class="button" href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GlassHouse97/AI-Cost-Efficiency-Portfolio-Project/main/DATA/TRACKS/portfolio-project/mru-portfolio-project.zip">Download portfolio project <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="repo-note">The <a href="https://github.com/GlassHouse97/AI-Cost-Efficiency-Portfolio-Project" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GitHub repository</a> connects both learning tracks with the completed dashboard and reproducible solution. The dashboard uses HTML, CSS, and JavaScript, with Python scripts for data preparation and validation. A Node.js container serves it on Google Cloud Run.</p>
      <p class="repo-note">I used Codex throughout the build to turn the business questions into implementation tasks, develop the analysis and dashboard, and work through validation. The project includes the assumptions, calculation code, and evidence tables so the results can be checked.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</ProjectLayout>

<style>
  .positive { color: #79e0ba; }
  .negative { color: #ff9b90; }
  .outcome-board { padding: clamp(1.4rem, 4vw, 2rem); border: 1px solid rgba(121, 224, 186, 0.3); border-radius: 22px; background: linear-gradient(145deg, #132c32, #0b1729 60%); box-shadow: var(--shadow); }
  .board-heading { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.65rem; justify-content: space-between; color: #79e0ba; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.6rem; }
  .board-heading small { color: var(--ink-muted); font-size: inherit; }
  .board-question { margin: 2rem 0; font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: clamp(2rem, 3vw, 2.7rem); line-height: 1.12; }
  .board-question em { color: #79e0ba; }
  .outcome-row { display: flex; gap: 1rem; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; padding: 1.3rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .outcome-row > span { display: grid; gap: 0.3rem; }
  .outcome-row b { color: var(--ink); font-size: 1rem; }
  .outcome-row strong { font-size: clamp(1.55rem, 2.5vw, 2.1rem); text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .outcome-row small { display: block; color: var(--ink-muted); font-size: 0.66rem; font-weight: 400; }
  .board-note { margin: 1rem 0 0; color: var(--ink-muted); font-size: 0.7rem; }
  .workflow-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 14px; margin-block: 2rem; }
  .workflow-grid article, .track-card { padding: 1.5rem; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 16px; background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.025); }
  .workflow-grid article > span, .mini-label { color: #79e0ba; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.68rem; }
  .workflow-grid h3 { margin: 1rem 0 0.6rem; font-size: 1rem; }
  .workflow-grid p { margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .kpi-list { margin-block: 1.5rem 2rem; padding-left: 1.4rem; }
  .kpi-list li { padding: 0.4rem 0 0.4rem 0.3rem; }
  .kpi-list li::marker { color: #79e0ba; }
  .kpi-list strong { color: var(--ink); }
  .table-scroll { max-width: 100%; overflow-x: auto; margin-block: 2rem; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 14px; }
  .table-scroll:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid #79e0ba; outline-offset: 4px; }
  table { width: 100%; min-width: 400px; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.88rem; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  caption { padding: 0.9rem 1.2rem; color: var(--ink-muted); background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.025); text-align: left; font-size: 0.7rem; }
  th, td { padding: 0.9rem 1.2rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  th { text-align: left; }
  td, thead th:not(:first-child) { text-align: right; white-space: nowrap; }
  tbody th { color: var(--ink-muted); font-weight: 400; }
  .sensitivity-panel { margin-top: 2rem; padding: 1.5rem; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 16px; background: rgba(121, 224, 186, 0.035); }
  .sensitivity-panel h3 { margin: 0.8rem 0 1.2rem; font-size: 1.15rem; }
  .range-row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.6rem; justify-content: space-between; padding-block: 0.65rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .range-row b { font-size: 0.85rem; }
  .sensitivity-panel p { margin: 1rem 0 0; font-size: 0.78rem; }
  .recommendations { padding-left: 1.5rem; margin: 0 0 2.5rem; }
  .recommendations li { padding: 0 0 1.3rem 0.4rem; }
  .recommendations li::marker { color: #79e0ba; font-family: var(--font-mono); }
  .track-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 16px; margin-top: 2rem; }
  .learner-steps { padding-left: 1.4rem; margin-block: 1.5rem 2rem; }
  .learner-steps li { padding-left: 0.3rem; }
  .learner-steps li::marker { color: #79e0ba; }
  .track-card { display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start; }
  .track-card h3 { margin: 0.9rem 0; font-size: 1.2rem; }
  .track-card p { font-size: 0.88rem; }
  .track-note { margin-top: auto; padding-block: 0.5rem 1rem; color: var(--ink-muted); font-size: 0.7rem; }
  .track-card .button { width: 100%; min-height: 48px; padding-inline: 0.85rem; font-size: 0.75rem; }
  .repo-note { margin-top: 1.5rem; font-size: 0.87rem; }
  .repo-note a { color: #79e0ba; }
  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .workflow-grid, .track-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .outcome-row small { font-size: 0.6rem; }
  }
</style>
